{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791572722,"nonce":"pFz2vDQHjTUEd9/4FbTu+ZL8g48=","title":"A stylesheet state rule resets a node to a snapshot that predates its bound writes","message":"A node that a :hover, :active or :focus rule applies to has its RenderProps snapshotted once in base_styles. When the state ends, the stylesheet pass restores that snapshot (renderer/stylesheet/selectors.rs apply_complex_selector_styles, and apply_state_styles for #id rules). Any write a signal binding made after the snapshot is overwritten with the old value.\n\nReproducer (blinc_layout, no widget involved): a div with class \"t\" and bg bound to a computed over a bool State; stylesheet \".t:hover { opacity: 0.5 }\". Hover the node, flip the State and apply the queued partial updates: the background shows the new colour. Move the pointer off the node and run apply_stylesheet_state_styles: the background is the colour from before the flip.\n\nEffect: a bound property and a stylesheet state rule cannot share a node. This is why the core widgets (toggle, checkbox, radio, button) re-read the stylesheet themselves in apply_css_overrides inside a Stateful instead of binding their colours, and why cn_styles says not to redeclare bg or border for them. It blocks retiring those closures (2c565dc) while keeping :hover rules in a stylesheet.\n\nDirection: the state pass should not own a snapshot of properties other writers also set. Either a bound write updates the node's entry in base_styles, or a state variant is applied as a layer over the current props and removed by value rather than by restoring a copy. The second is the form that fits per-node interaction signals (Interaction, 2c565dc) and the tiers in 7cabafe.\n\nNot fixed. Needs a decision on how the layers are stored.","files":null}]}